# Git guesses text-vs-binary from a file's first few thousand bytes, and
# for one of our PDF fixtures it guessed wrong: pr6531_2.pdf happens to
# contain no NUL early on, so git filed it as text. On a Windows checkout
# with core.autocrlf=true — the default on GitHub's Windows runners — that
# means every LF becomes CRLF, which shifts every byte offset after the
# first newline. A PDF's cross-reference table is a list of byte offsets,
# so the file arrives structurally broken: "invalid file trailer", on
# Windows only, from a file that is byte-perfect in the repository.
#
# Marking the formats explicitly is the fix, and it has to be explicit —
# a heuristic that is right most of the time is exactly what produced a
# platform-specific failure in the first place.
*.pdf binary
*.wasm binary
*.woff2 binary
*.otf binary
*.ttf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.dmg binary
*.exe binary
*.dylib binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
